The inverse limit, with the subspace topology inherited from the product of the stage Scott topologies, is an injective space. This is the injectivity step of Scott's Proposition 4.1. Lemma 3.9 supplies compatibility of adjacent extended coordinates.

Given a dense embedding e of X into Y and a continuous map f from X into the inverse limit, extend each coordinate of f along e by the Scott extension. Each extended coordinate is continuous. Lemma 3.9, applied to two consecutive coordinates of f together with their compatibility, makes the extended coordinates compatible. The resulting map into the inverse limit is continuous because its composite with the inclusion into the product is the continuous tuple of those coordinate extensions. On X, each coordinate of the extension agrees with f by the identity property of Scott extension for a continuous map.
